# Secrets for the Edge Functions. Copy to supabase/functions/.env for local
# development; on a hosted project use `supabase secrets set` instead.
#
# EVERYTHING IN THIS FILE IS OPTIONAL. With none of it set, `npm test` passes,
# the apps build, and the 2-2-2 app's places feature works with venues typed by
# hand — the `places` function answers "no capabilities" and every search
# control hides itself. That is a supported configuration, not a degraded one,
# and it is enforced by tests/guards/maps-optional.test.ts.

# --- The places proxy ------------------------------------------------------
# Google Cloud console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials.
#
# Enable exactly three APIs on the project and restrict the key to them:
#   Places API (New)   — venue search
#   Routes API         — drive time for getaways
#   Maps Static API    — the preview thumbnail
#
# Restrict it by API, not by IP: Edge Functions have no static egress address,
# so an IP restriction cannot work here. The real abuse controls are the JWT
# check in the function and the per-couple daily cap in `places_usage`.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Requests per couple per day before the function answers `rate_limited`.
# Defaults to 100 when unset. Every request past this is a line on somebody's
# bill, so the cap exists to make a loop in a screen a bug rather than an
# invoice.
PLACES_DAILY_LIMIT=100

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS NOT IN apps/two-two-two/.env
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ values are compiled into the app bundle, which ships to phones
# and can be read back out of it. That is fine for the Supabase anon key,
# because row-level security is what protects the data and every request it
# makes is still subject to it.
#
# A mapping key has no RLS behind it. Possession *is* the authorization, and
# every request made with it is billed to whoever owns it. So it lives here,
# where it is a server-side secret the app never sees — the app asks the
# function what it can do rather than reading a key to find out.
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored with `!.env.example`; never `git add -f`
# an env file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
